# Probabilistic Programming

This chapter is a shallow dive into Probabilistic programming using the SciPy library [https://scipy.org](https://scipy.org) and [TensorFlow Probability](https://www.tensorflow.org/probability). A warning to you, dear reader: the material in this chapter represents topics that I have never used to any large extent professionally, unlike the rest of this book that represents topics that I both experiment with on my own time and use professionally. I hope that this short chapter is interesting to read and directs you to material for further study.

Usually you would study probabalistic programming after learning statistics and statistical machine learning, topics I am not covering in this book. I will try to make this chapter a self-contained introduction that will be sufficient for you to learn and decide if you want to dive deeper.

Probabilistic Programming is used for classification and models that exploit the probability distributions over data. We can think of a probability distribution as a function that assigns probabilbities to different possible outcomes, given a current state. Outcomes of random processes are expressed as *random variables* that will each conform to some type of probability distribution.

A sample space is a set of all possible outcomes. For drawing a playing card from a deck of 52 cards, the sample space is simply a set with the 52 card values. Initially the probability of drawing a Jack of Hearts (or any other card) is 1/52. Initially the probability of drawing a Heart is 1/4. If there is prior data, such that a 8 of Hearts was just drawn and onkly 51 cards remain in the deck then these probabilities change. Probability distributions can be discreet or continuous. We will look at a code example for this later.

With Bayesian Inference, the probability of the likelihood of future events is estimated. Suppose that you were asked to inspect a pound of pinto beans and discard small stones that might look like pinto beans. One person tells you that there are almost never any stones in a bag of beans and someone else tells you that he usually finds two or three stones in a one pound bag. Given this task, you have the expectation that you might likely find at least one stone. It takes too long to pick up and examine each bean so you decide to move the beans around with your fingers looking for stones. After a minute you find a stone and throw it away. Should you stop searching? Intuition tells us that at least two things: the chance of finding a stone in a second minute of searching will be smaller than finding on in the first minute, and the longer we search without finding a stone or another stone then the more confidence we have that we probably found all of the stones in a pount of pinto beans.

## Examples Using SciPy

SciPy supports more than 100 different [probability distributions](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/stats.html). The script **norm_dist.py** shows an example for normal distributions:

```python
import scipy.stats
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy

n1 = scipy.stats.norm(0.0, 1.0) # mean at 0.0, standard deviation of 1.0
print(f"mean={n1.mean()}, std={n1.std()}, probability density function={n1.pdf(0)}")

x = numpy.linspace(-2, 2, 3000)
mean = 0
sigma = 1

probability_density_function = scipy.stats.norm.pdf(x, mean, sigma)
cumulative_distribution_function = scipy.stats.norm.cdf(x, mean, sigma)

plt.plot(x, probability_density_function, label='Probability Density Function')
plt.plot(x, cumulative_distribution_function,
         label='Cumulative Distribution Function')
plt.legend();
plt.show()
```

Printed output and plot:

```
mean=0.0,  std=1.0,  probability density function=0.3989422804014327
```

![](normdist.png)

Joint probabilities involve events (variables values) that are not independent. If we have two variables X and Y then the joint probability for events X and Y is : **P(X, Y) = P(X | Y)** that you would read as *the probability of events X and Y occuring at the same time*. Similarly a conditional probability is defined as the probability of event X occurring when event Y has occured. Marginal probability is the probability of events X and Y happening when there is no connection between X and Y, that is that they are independent. An example of conditional probability is *what is the probability that a person rides a horse, given that they live in Arizona?* compared to the simple probability *what is the probability that a person rides a horse*.

Probability variables can be observed in which case we know their current values or unobserved (or hidden) variables in which case we use information of prior distributions of the hidden variables.

Probalistic programming is developing an understanding of the relationships between input and output data, building parameterized models of these relationships, estimate good model parameters, and finally to be able to make predictions. Sometimes the parameterized models will represent real physical phenomena like calculating wind resistence when driving a car. We might build a model that includes constant parameters that scale linear car speed, log of car speed, and the square of car speed and then use prior data to estimate good values for these parameters. Another use case might be an AI agent navigating in a virtual world with actions that move the agent, affect the state of the environment, etc. that we will discuss in the next chapter.

We want to develop an intuition for thinking in terms of probabilities and mapping problems to a form where we can use the ScyPi library of or libraries like PyMC that are specifically designed for probabilistic programming. We will work through a few simple problems using ScyPi and by the end of this chapter you will have enough exeperience to decide if you want to dig further into this topic either because of general interest or specific use cases in your work that can be solved with ScyPi, PyMC, and other commonly used libraries.

You may already be familiar with Bayes Rule that allows us to estimate probabilities from observed data:

**P(θ | X) = P(X | θ) * P(θ) / P(X)**

This reads as "The probability of θ being true given that X is true is equal to the probability of X being true given that θ is true, times the probability that θ is true, divided by the probability the X is true."

The term P(θ | X) is reffered to as the posterior, P(X | θ) is called the likelyhood, P(θ) is called the prior, and P(X) is called the evidence.

We are not making absolute statements about the values of random variables. Instead we think of random variables of having values in a given probability distribution, like the normal distribution in the last data plot. One characteristic of probability distributions is that the integral of the entire distribution always sums to 1.0 which simply means that the probabilities of choosing all possible values in the distribution sum to 1.0.

